#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055927 :tutorial/content "We're now able to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\ln   ( x ) ) = \\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we set \n\n$$\ny = \\ln   ( x ) .\n$$ \nOur goal is to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, note that since $y = \\ln   ( x ) ,$ we have\n\n$$\ne^y = x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x}} ( {e}^{y} ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x}} ( x ) \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} y}} ( {e}^{y} ) & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} \\cdot  {e}^{y} & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {e}^{y}}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $e^y = x,$ we conclude\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac1x\n$$\nas required." :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Natural Logarithm"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055945 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to **plot** the graph of $y = x + 2.$ We can do this by substituting some $x$-values into the equation, calculating the corresponding $y$-values, and plotting the $xy$-pairs. \n\nWe'll start by creating a table of values for $x$ and $y$, including a few values of $x$ to begin with.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing our equation $y=x+2,$ we can calculate the corresponding values for $y.$ So for $x=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\ny = -5+2 = {\\color{blue}{-3}},\n$$\n\nand we add this to our table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{-3}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe repeat this process to fill in the rest of the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/56/5677e939189394e8f98782bb5071730d4c916fe65857d673d887e0063d2ebce6.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a **straight line**.\n\n![](images/34/341d9dea9be40c6fd4065d20711f58c66b4a3cb4c667e3c100a08674d66041b3.png)\n\nThis is the graph of $y = x + 2.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055946 :tutorial/content "The graph of a linear equation will *always* be a straight line! As we know, any straight line is defined by two points. \nTherefore, instead of plotting lots of points, we only need to plot two of them, and then we can draw a straight line through them. \n\nFor example, we previously encountered a line that passed through the points $(0,-2),$ $(1,1),$ $(2,4),$ and $(3,7).$ Its graph is shown below: \n\n![](images/d2/d2d8891102ce99020f544bd8d82a5ff0454080474ce4267ef5285db92f5b2ca0.png)\n\nHowever, given only two points $(0, -2)$ and $(3, 7)$, we can still draw the same line through them:\n\n![](images/65/6594282ddc3da3f96f2fb9983835776cd61e429188115361241a39f53b49d22d.png)" :tutorial/title "A Line Passing Through Two Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055989 :tutorial/content "Functions are usually given in the form $y=f(x)$ where $y$ is explicitly written as a function of $x.$ However, they can also be written in **implicit** form, like\n$$\n2x^3+y^2=3.\n$$\n\nWhen a function is given in implicit form, we can differentiate it using the method of **implicit differentiation**, which consists of the following two steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x.$\n\nWe need to remember that $y$ *is a function of* $x,$ i.e., $y = y(x),$ So, we apply the chain rule:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} + {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 3 ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {y}^{2} ) &  = 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} y} ( {y}^{2} ) ( \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} ) &  = 0 \\\\ 6 {x}^{2} + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 6 {x}^{2} + 2 y {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 y {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = - 6 {x}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 {x}^{2}}{y}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the derivative of $y$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=- \\dfrac{3x^2}{y}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060876 :tutorial/content "The Pythagorean identity is\n\n$$\n\\cos^2{\\theta}+\\sin^2{\\theta}=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to simplify trigonometric expressions. For example, let's try to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\theta \\left(\\sin \\theta + \\dfrac {\\cos^2 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe first expand the parentheses, and then simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }) & =2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +2\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =2({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n2(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta) =2\\cdot 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\theta \\left(\\sin \\theta + \\dfrac {\\cos^2 \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\\right) = 2.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060907 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given two vectors \n$\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -4,4 \\rangle,$ shown below. How do we compute the vectors $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$  and  $-5\\mathbf{a}$ in terms of the standard basis $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}?$\n\n![](images/25/254809cbc834b493759a6fa09222a2c90c5fbfc3280d22e82470e760a7a4c508.png)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we collect all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s. We know that $\\mathbf{a} = {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}-4}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}4}\\mathbf{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{i}+{\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{j})+({\\color{red}-4}\\mathbf{i}+{\\color{blue}4}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =({\\color{red}2}+({\\color{red}-4}))\\mathbf{i}+({\\color{blue}3}+{\\color{blue}4})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}-2}\\mathbf{i}+{\\color{blue}7}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get \n$$\n\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}-2} \\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}7}\\mathbf{j} =  \\langle {\\color{red}-2}, {\\color{blue}7} \\rangle = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}- 2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}7}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $-5\\mathbf{a},$ we multiply each of the components by $-5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\mathbf{a} & =-5({\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{i}+{\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(-5\\cdot {\\color{red}2})\\mathbf{i}+(-5\\cdot {\\color{blue}3})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={\\color{red}-10}\\mathbf{i}+{\\color{blue}-15}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get \n$$\n-5\\mathbf{a} =  {\\color{red}-10} \\mathbf{i}+ {\\color{blue}-15} \\mathbf{j} = \\langle {\\color{red}-10}, {\\color{blue}-15} \\rangle = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}- 10} \\\\ {\\color{blue}- 15}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060908 :tutorial/content "To add or subtract vectors given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to add or subtract each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{\\color{red}2},{\\color{blue}5}⟩+⟨{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{blue}7}⟩ &= ⟨{\\color{red}2}+({\\color{red}-3}),{\\color{blue}5}+{\\color{blue}7}⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{blue}12}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}5}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}-3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}7}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2}+({\\color{red}-3}) \\\\ {\\color{blue}5}+{\\color{blue}7}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}-1} \\\\ {\\color{blue}12}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, to multiply a vector given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to multiply each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot ⟨{\\color{red}2},{\\color{blue}5}⟩ &= ⟨(-5)\\cdot {\\color{red}2},(-5)\\cdot {\\color{blue}5}⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨{\\color{red}-10},{\\color{blue}-25}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}5}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-5)\\cdot {\\color{red}2} \\\\ (-5)\\cdot {\\color{blue}5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}-10} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-25}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Angle Bracket or Column Vector Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060909 :tutorial/content "To denote a vector, we have used two notations:\n\n- a single bold lowercase letter, such as $\\mathbf{v},$ or\n\n- two uppercase letters representing the points of the ends of the vector, with an arrow over them, such as $\\overrightarrow{AB}.$\n\nWe will mostly stick to these notations. However, there are many possible notations for vectors. For example, sometimes a vector can be denoted as\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{V}, \\quad v, \\quad \\hat{v}.\n$$\n\nThe rightmost notation, $\\hat{v},$ is usually used to represent unit vectors in particular." :tutorial/title "Common Notations Used When Describing Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060910 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nExpressed in component form, our vector is $\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i} + 4 \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nTo find the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a},$ we can use the Pythagorean theorem on $\\triangle OXA.$\n\nThe legs of the triangle have lengths\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{OX}| & =|-2|=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{XA}| & =|\\,4\\,|=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{|\\overset{\\to }{OX}{|}^{2}+|\\overset{\\to }{XA}{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060911 :tutorial/content "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ was given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\,\\mathbf{j}=\\langle a_x,a_y \\rangle = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{a}_{x} \\\\ {a}_{y}\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $|| \\mathbf a ||$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." :tutorial/title "General Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060920 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we know that the dot product can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\cdot \\cos\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point.\n\nHowever, there is another formula for the dot product that we can use if the two vectors are given in component form. If the components of our two vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}a_x},{\\color{blue}a_y}, a_z \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}b_x},{\\color{blue}b_y}, b_z \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthen the dot product can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}a_x} \\cdot {\\color{red}b_x} + {\\color{blue}a_y} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}b_y} + a_z \\cdot b_z\n$$\n\nFor example, if we have two vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5},2 \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6},7 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nThen we can find the dot product of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4}+({\\color{blue}-5})\\cdot {\\color{blue}6}+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-30+14 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The procedure for computing the dot product works for vectors of any number of components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5}⟩\\cdot ⟨{\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6}⟩ & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4}+({\\color{blue}-5})\\cdot {\\color{blue}6} \\\\  & =12-30 \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5},2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨{\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6},7,9⟩ & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4}+({\\color{blue}-5})\\cdot {\\color{blue}6}+2\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =12-30+14+9 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060921 :tutorial/content "To understand the reasoning behind the formula for the dot product of two vectors, it's helpful to write the two vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors and carry out the computation the long way.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 3,-5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,6 \\rangle.$\n\nExpressing the vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a} &=3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using properties of the dot product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =({\\color{red}3}\\mathbf{i}+({\\color{blue}-5})\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ({\\color{red}4}\\mathbf{i}+{\\color{blue}6}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}+({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular, meaning the angle between them is $\\theta=90^\\circ$ and consequently their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since the angle between a vector and itself is $\\theta=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{i} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =j\\cdot i=0, \\\\ i\\cdot i & =j\\cdot j=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand our computation reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\overbrace{\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}}^{=1}+({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\overbrace{\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}}^{=0}+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\overbrace{\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}}^{=0}+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\overbrace{\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}}^{=1} \\\\  & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\cdot 1+({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\cdot 0+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\cdot 0+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =⟨{\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5}⟩\\cdot ⟨{\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6}⟩ \\\\  & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Formula"}]}
